steveyo wrote:
> There's this nagging in the back of my brain to try for the devnull
> tourney's "Fastest Ascension" category this year.  (Rast! Darn you!)
>
> An open discussion of speed strategies would be interesting (to me, at
> least).
>
> Some random thoughts on a low-turns Nethack game...
> - a character which starts with speed
> - plan for less back-tracking
> - digging tool
> - get speed boots (or haste-self spell)
> - magic mapping spell (or magic markers to write the scroll)
> - get the Eye of Aethiopica (dungeon-branch teleport)
> - get the Orb of Fate (level teleport)
> - get a pet Archon to kill most foes instantly
> - condense things into fewer moves, if possible (i.e. looting bags,
> autopickup strategies)

The hardest part to get used to is not fully exploring every level.
Needless to say, you will want to skip Sokoban, but you will most
likely also want to skip searching a dead-end corridor for the door to
that last room you /know/ is there.

Some other useful items:

- a =oSD reduces turns spent eating
- jumping boots are better than speed boots in the planes

Monks start with speed, but wizards can caste Haste Self hungerlessly
(i.e. almost continuously after obtaining the Eye).

Microtactics could help, but might just be a waste of time. If there's
a doorway like this:

######
-----+----
..........

which you know you'll be passing through more than once, then it may be
a worthwhile investment of turns to kick it down and pass through
diagonally. Unfortunately, a previous post by Dylan
(<86pszplvzq.fsf@strackenz.spod-central.org>) tells us:

> The chance of any unlocked door not sticking is dependent on your Luck
> and the average of your strength, dexterity, and constitution.
> The chance of kicking open a door is dependent on the same factors (and
> may receive a bonus if you're a martial artist) but is somewhat less
> (using rnl(35) compared to opening's rnl(20)). No counter is kept of
> either type of attempt: each action has an entirely independent
> chance.

If you want to *really* get into anal-retentive microtactics (and why
the hell not? Nethack is an intellectual exercise as much as anything
else!), you could write a simple program using nethack's door-kicking
algorithm which takes your St, Dx, Co, and a guess at your luck, and
tells you the expected number of moves it will take to open the door.

There are countless examples of microtactics saving a move or two, but
each will require the same detailed approach. I can imagine the Nethack
Speedrunner's Toolbox, a suite of small apps written to tell you stuff
like whether it's worth kicking that door down.

I am skeptical that such a detailed approach will pay off in terms of
real-world time spent. Speed runs are dominated by pure luck, i.e.
whether you find a /oW on Dlvl1.

